WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:20.080
<v Ira Glass>Back in the 1970s, the Atlanta Journal Newspaper several times a week for years published this column called the Georgia Rambler. Charles Salter wrote the column. Basically he would get up in the morning, say goodbye to his wife, Sally, get in his car, and head out on the road to some small town, sometimes without much of a plan at all, not even sure where he's going to end up.
<v Ira Glass>在20世纪70年代，亚特兰大《亚特兰大新闻报》多年来每周数次刊登一个名为“乔治亚漫游者”的专栏。查尔斯·索尔特是该专栏的作者。基本上，他每天早上起床，跟妻子萨莉告别，坐上他的车，前往某个小镇，有时根本没有什么计划，甚至不知道自己最终会到哪里。

00:00:20.080 --> 00:01:00.470
<v Charles Salter>I would look at a Georgia highway map. And Sally would say, honey, where are you going this morning? And I said, I think I'll go east. And I'd would go over to a town 50, 80, 100 miles from Atlanta, and I would see the county agent, or the cafe where the guys and gals would gather to drink coffee and talk. And I'd say you all have probably read Rita's Digest. For a long term they had an article called The Most Unforgettable Person I Ever Met. Well who in your town, who's the most unforgettable person you ever met? Who would you talk about if he were at a convention 1,000 miles away. I wish you fellas could known so and so back home.
<v Charles Salter>我会看一张乔治亚州公路地图。萨莉会问，亲爱的，你今天早上要去哪儿？我说，我想去东边。我会去距离亚特兰大50、80或100英里外的一个城镇，我会见当地的农业推广员，或者去那些男男女女聚在一起喝咖啡聊天的小餐馆。我会问，你们大概都读过《读者文摘》吧。他们长期以来有个专栏叫《我所见过最难忘的人》。那么在你们镇上，谁是你见过最难忘的人？如果他在1000英里外的某个会议上，你会谈论谁？我真希望你们这些伙计能认识家乡的某某人。

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:06.790
<v Charles Salter>I was seeking good stories from ordinary folks.
<v Charles Salter>我当时在寻找普通民众的好故事。

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:26.440
<v Ira Glass>There was the 55-year-old man who never wore shoes, and the pharmacist who discovered in an old ledger there seemed to be a recipe for Coca-Cola. And the country father of five who told the Georgia Rambler that when he goes to the sales barn to buy a small calf, "I call it buying me a lawn mower. When the grass dies down thanks to the calf, I kill my lawnmower and eat it".
<v Ira Glass>比如那个从不穿鞋的55岁男人，还有那个在旧账本里发现好像有可口可乐配方的药剂师。还有那个有五个孩子的乡下父亲，他告诉“乔治亚漫游者”，当他去牲口集市买小牛时，“我管这叫买割草机。当小牛把草吃光后，我就杀掉我的割草机并吃掉它。”

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:39.220
<v Ira Glass>There's Pete Ware, Justice of the Peace in Troup County, who said that he did 40% of his weddings after midnight, including shotgun weddings. Though he complained to the Georgia Rambler that people these days are too lazy to bring shotguns to their shotgun weddings.
<v Ira Glass>还有特鲁普县的太平绅士皮特·韦尔，他说他在午夜后处理了40%的婚礼，包括“枪口下的婚礼”。尽管他向“乔治亚漫游者”抱怨，现在的人太懒了，甚至懒得带猎枪参加他们的“枪口下的婚礼”。

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.310
<v Charles Salter>And also was lucky enough to hear about haunted houses.
<v Charles Salter>而且我还很幸运地听到了关于闹鬼房子的故事。

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.980
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I've been reading your columns and it seems like a there's a lot of haunted houses in Georgia.
<v Ira Glass>是啊，我一直在读你的专栏，看起来乔治亚州有很多闹鬼的房子。

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:55.300
<v Charles Salter>Yes, and I had a lot a funny interviewing a woman who was in the Marine Corps with Lee Harvey Oswald. And down in [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Charles Salter>是的，我采访一位曾经与李·哈维·奥斯瓦尔德一起在海军陆战队服役的女性时，觉得非常有趣。在[听不清]……

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.130
<v Ira Glass>I sort of forgot he was trained as a Marine.
<v Ira Glass>我倒是忘了他也曾受训成为一名海军陆战队员。

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:20.540
<v Charles Salter>Yes. And his nickname was the creep. And they were on the rifle range one day, and the devil winds were blowing out of the desert. I forget the word for it, the Spanish word, and he was still shooting while the others were smoking cigarettes and drinking coffee. Because they couldn't shoot accurately with the wind blowing from the side. And he kept hitting the bull's eye.
<v Charles Salter>是的。他的绰号叫“怪胎”。有一天他们在射击场，沙漠里刮着魔鬼风。我忘了那个词怎么说了，是西班牙语，当其他人在抽烟喝咖啡时，他还在射击。因为侧风太大，其他人无法精准射击。而他却一直命中靶心。

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:56.210
<v Ira Glass>OK, full disclosure, Charles' son Chuck is married one of the producers of our radio show, Lisa Pollak, which is how we heard about the Georgia Rambler. And when all of us here at our radio show-- you're listening to This American Life by the way from WBEZ Chicago disturbed by Public Radio international-- when all of us heard about the Georgia Rambler, and this idea that you would get out of the city and just drive into some small town at random, walk around, and talk to people until you find a story, and get all these people from these towns with just a couple hundred a couple thousand people all over Georgia talking about life where they live, it just seemed like a really fun thing to try.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，披露一下，查尔斯的儿子查克娶了我们广播节目的制片人之一丽莎·波拉克，我们就是这样听说“乔治亚漫游者”的。当我们要在这里做广播节目时——顺便说一下，你正在收听的是由WBEZ芝加哥制作、国际公共广播电台发行的《美国生活》——当听到“乔治亚漫游者”以及那种走出城市，随机开车去某个小镇，四处走走，与人交谈直到找到故事的想法时，听到这些来自乔治亚州各地的、只有几百或几千人口的小镇居民讲述他们生活的地方，这听起来是一件非常有趣的事情，值得一试。

00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:25.460
<v Ira Glass>So these last few weeks, nine of us flew down in Georgia. It was producers of our radio show plus comedian and author Eugene Mirman, and we brought the son of the Georgia Rambler, Chuck. There are a 159 counties in Georgia. We picked nine counties from a hat. We used an Atlanta Braves baseball cap for that. Each of us went to a different county, spend just a day of two, and we ended up with so many stories that we can't come close to fitting them all into one show. Though we can fit seven, which we bring you today.
<v Ira Glass>所以在过去的几周里，我们九个人飞到了乔治亚州。其中包括我们广播节目的制片人、喜剧演员兼作家尤金·米尔曼，我们还带上了“乔治亚漫游者”的儿子查克。乔治亚州有159个县。我们用帽子抽签选出了九个县。我们为此用了一顶亚特兰大勇士队的棒球帽。我们每个人去了不同的县，只待了一两天，结果我们得到了太多的故事，根本没法塞进一期节目里。虽然我们可以选出七个，今天就带给你们。

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:37.280
<v Ira Glass>And we'll just kick things off here. We'll start things off with Dave Kestenbaum, who's usually a correspondent for NPR's Planet Money, who we took off the economics beat and sent to Georgia to look for some story or person that is unforgettable.
<v Ira Glass>我们先从这里开始。我们从戴夫·克斯坦鲍姆开始，他通常是美国国家公共电台（NPR）《星球经济》的记者，我们让他暂时放下经济新闻，派他去乔治亚州寻找一个难忘的故事或人物。

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.320
<v Ira Glass>And Dave, you went to Meriwether County, right near the western border of the state.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫，你去了梅里韦瑟县，就在该州的西侧边界，对吧？

00:03:41.320 --> 00:04:06.190
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Yes, and the people of Meriwether County were extremely friendly. They were happy to give directions or recommend a place for lunch. But they were completely at a loss when I asked the one question that mattered. Who is the most unforgettable person in town? "Unforgettable", people would say, "In this town"? Someone showed me a phone book. It was thinner than your finger. The Rs, Ss, and Ts were all on the same page.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>是的，梅里韦瑟县的人们非常友好。他们很乐意指路或推荐午餐地点。但当我问出那个最关键的问题时，他们完全不知所措。谁是镇上最难忘的人？“难忘？”人们会说，“在这个镇上？”有人给我看了电话簿。它比你的手指还薄。R、S和T都在同一页上。

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:22.330
<v Dave Kestenbaum>There was one person who came up over and over again, lots of people mentioned his name. The gentleman in question is dead. Though I suppose if people talk about you after you die, that's pretty much the definition of unforgettable. People said he helped the poor, he was a good, good man, and everyone knew him by his initials,
<v Dave Kestenbaum>有一个人被反复提到，很多人都提到了他的名字。这位先生已经去世了。不过我想，如果人们在你死后还会谈论你，这几乎就是“难忘”的定义了。人们说他帮助穷人，是个非常好的人，大家都用他的名字缩写来称呼他……

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.200
<v Franklin Delano Roosevelt>My friends and neighbors--
<v Franklin Delano Roosevelt>我的朋友们和邻居们……

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.250
<v Dave Kestenbaum>FDR.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>FDR（富兰克林·德拉诺·罗斯福）。

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:35.030
<v Franklin Delano Roosevelt>--11 years ago, I came to live at Warm Springs for the first time.
<v Franklin Delano Roosevelt>……11年前，我第一次来到温泉区居住。

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:56.410
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Franklin Delano Roosevelt came to Warm Springs, Georgia 41 times. Yes, people count. He set up a polio treatment center here. The locals say proudly, this was a place he could relax. He would get a fiddle player to come and play at night. But Sylvia Bishop Wright, she lives in an area called the Cove, she told me FDR liked to do something else, something you don't read in the history books.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>富兰克林·德拉诺·罗斯福曾41次来到乔治亚州的温泉区。是的，人们都记着呢。他在这里建立了一个小儿麻痹症治疗中心。当地人自豪地说，这是一个他可以放松的地方。他会让小提琴手晚上来演奏。但住在被称为“海湾”地区的西尔维娅·毕肖普·赖特告诉我，FDR还喜欢做另一件事，那是你在历史书中读不到的。

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:02.740
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>When Roosevelt was coming down here, he especially liked the moonshine in the Cove.
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>罗斯福来这里时，他特别喜欢“海湾”地区的私酿威士忌。

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:04.030
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Roosevelt liked Moonshine?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>罗斯福喜欢私酿威士忌？

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:08.600
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>They called it stump juice because they'd hide it in the old stumps.
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>他们管那叫“树桩果汁”，因为他们把它藏在旧树桩里。

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.150
<v Dave Kestenbaum>In a tree stump?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>在树桩里？

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:24.000
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>In a tree stump. They'd just hide it down there. Am I telling things I shouldn't be telling?
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>在树桩里。他们就把酒藏在那下面。我是不是说了不该说的话？

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:55.270
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I was pretty sure this didn't count. You can't go to some small town in search of someone unforgettable and come back with Franklin Delano Roosevelt, the former President of the United States, someone everyone already knows. But then I thought wait, Roosevelt would have been drinking during prohibition when it was illegal to sell alcohol, illegal the manufacturer it, to transport it, we wrote it into our constitution which FDR swore in his oath of office to preserve, protect, and defend. I got more interested when Sylvia left me a voicemail later that day.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我很确定这不算数。你不能去某个小镇寻找难忘的人，结果带回来的却是富兰克林·德拉诺·罗斯福，这位美国前总统，一个大家都已经认识的人。但后来我想，等等，罗斯福喝这些酒是在禁酒令时期，当时销售酒精是非法的，制造、运输都是非法的，我们把它写进了宪法，而FDR在就职誓词中发誓要维护、保护和捍卫它。当我那天晚些时候收到西尔维娅的语音留言时，我变得更感兴趣了。

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:19.720
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>They David, hey. This is Sylvia at the newspaper. And you know how you get to thinking about things. You know when we were talking about the booze, you know the stump juice and all, and about how Roosevelt liked it. But I'm telling you, I don't want anything said about Roosevelt that would be wrong. These folks down here will fight you about Franklin D. Roosevelt.
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>喂，戴维，你好。我是报社的西尔维娅。你知道你怎么会去想那些事。你知道我们谈论酒的时候，你知道“树桩果汁”之类的，还有罗斯福是怎么喜欢它的。但我告诉你，我不希望关于罗斯福有任何错误的说法。这里的人会为了富兰克林·D·罗斯福跟你拼命的。

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:25.800
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Then silence like she'd covered up the mouth piece. There was a second voicemail from her.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>接着是一阵沉默，好像她捂住了话筒。她又发来了第二条语音留言。

00:06:25.800 --> 00:07:00.060
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>David, this is Sylvia again. I was interrupted right in the middle of my message. But like I said, these people down here love Franklin D. Roosevelt. You know his little white house is about three miles down the road. And I guess that's why people knew him on such a personal basis because he was such a good man and so friendly. And they loved him as president, absolutely. He came down here before he was president also and made friends with everybody. But I just wanted to make sure nothing bad was said about FDR.
<v Sylvia Bishop Wright>戴维，我是西尔维娅。我的留言被打断了。但正如我所说，这里的人非常爱戴富兰克林·D·罗斯福。你知道他那个小白屋就在三英里外的路上。我想这就是为什么人们对他有那种私下的了解，因为他是个好人，也非常友善。作为总统，他们绝对爱戴他。他当总统前也来过这里，和每个人都成了朋友。我只是想确保没有人说FDR的坏话。

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:28.330
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I went to that place that Sylvia mentioned, the little white house where FDR used to stay. He actually died here. The place is a museum now. And I asked about FDR and moonshine. Two guys told me, "Yeah, that's the local legend". But they didn't know if it was true. Clearly this is not a question historians have devoted much effort to answering. It's the 1930s version of did you inhale. Did you, sir, drink moonshine. Well, did you?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我去了西尔维娅提到的那个地方，就是FDR曾经住过的小白屋。他实际上死在这里。现在这里成了博物馆。我问起FDR和私酿威士忌的事。两个人告诉我：“是的，那是当地的传说。”但他们不知道是否属实。显然，历史学家并没有花太多精力去回答这个问题。这简直是1930年代版的“你吸入了吗？”。先生，您喝过私酿威士忌吗？好吧，你喝过吗？

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:48.330
<v Dave Kestenbaum>So now I found myself driving on the back roads into the Georgia woods to see if some president drank moonshine. I stopped at a small house this looked like it had definitely been there since FDR died. A tiny Porsche with three people just sitting on it. I waved as I walked up. They waved back. There was an old man, an older woman, and a younger guy, Roland Brown
<v Dave Kestenbaum>所以现在我发现自己正开着车穿过乔治亚州树林里的后路，去看看某位总统是否喝过私酿威士忌。我在一个小房子前停下，那房子看起来肯定在FDR去世时就已经在了。一辆微型保时捷，上面坐着三个人。我走过去时挥了挥手。他们也挥手回应。有一个老人、一位年长的妇女和一个年轻人，罗兰·布朗。

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.430
<v Roland Brown>This is the cove. This area is where they call the cove.
<v Roland Brown>这就是海湾。这个地区被称为海湾。

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.240
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I took a breath and asked did FDR come here to get moonshine?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我深吸了一口气，问FDR是不是来这里买私酿威士忌的？

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.480
<v Georgia Cove Resident>Well I wasn't here. I couldn't tell you about that.
<v Georgia Cove Resident>嗯，我当时不在。我可没法告诉你那个。

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.680
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Is that something you've heard?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这是你听说过的事吗？

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:22.200
<v Georgia Cove Resident>Well, I ain't going to tell you that. I don't know. [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]. You could go over the mountain and talked to Mr. Bunn-Wright's daughter and she might tell you. Mr. Bunn-Wright played the fiddle for him. He lived out here. One of Mr. Bunn-Wright's daughters is the only one left living I think.
<v Georgia Cove Resident>嗯，我不会告诉你那个的。我不知道。[听不清的短语]。你可以翻过山去跟邦恩·赖特先生的女儿谈谈，她可能会告诉你。邦恩·赖特先生曾为他拉过小提琴。他住在这里。我想邦恩·赖特先生的女儿中只有一位还健在。

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.790
<v Dave Kestenbaum>And the woman's name I'm looking for?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我正在找的那位女士的名字是？

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:31.720
<v Georgia Cove Resident>Aunt Nee is what we call her, Aunt Nee Newman. I don't even really know what her first name is.
<v Georgia Cove Resident>我们叫她尼阿姨，尼·纽曼阿姨。我甚至不知道她的名字叫什么。

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:44.180
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I drove over the mountains and found Aunt Nee's house. The doors were locked, no cars. I stood there for a minute knocking, trying all of the doors, but the house with silent. I gave up and drove away.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我翻过山找到了尼阿姨的家。门锁着，没有车。我在那里敲了一会儿门，试了所有的门，但房子里一片寂静。我放弃了，开车离开了。

00:08:44.180 --> 00:09:01.470
<v Dave Kestenbaum>On the right side of the road, I saw a woman working in a field. What the hell, I thought, I rolled down the window. "Do know someone named Aunt Nee"? "Aunt Nee? Yes", she said. She's my Aunt Nee". The woman pointed back to the house on the hill where I just knocked on the door. "Aunt Nee", she explained "is hard of hearing".
<v Dave Kestenbaum>在路右边，我看到一个女人在田里干活。管他呢，我想，我摇下车窗问：“你认识一个叫尼阿姨的人吗？”“尼阿姨？是的，”她说，“她是我的尼阿姨。”那个女人指了指我刚才敲过门的山上的房子。“尼阿姨，”她解释说，“听力不好。”

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:11.200
<v Linda Carpenter>I might not get her to the door. That white house right up on top of the hill. And she's 90 going on 91. I might have to go up and get her to the door.
<v Linda Carpenter>我可能没法让她来开门。就是山顶上那座白房子。她90岁了，快91了。我可能得上去让她来开门。

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.230
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Do you think you could come with me? That would be wonderful.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你觉得你能跟我一起去吗？那太好了。

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.090
<v Linda Carpenter>I'll walk you there.
<v Linda Carpenter>我陪你走过去。

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:14.910
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Thank you.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>谢谢。

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:43.500
<v Ira Glass>The woman whose name is Linda Carpenter, took me back to the house that seemed empty. Inside in near darkness in an armchair was Aunt Nee doing some sort of puzzle from a magazine. Aunt Nee's full name is Cornelia Wright Newman. Aunt Nee told me yes, she met President Roosevelt when she was a girl. FDR loved her father's fiddle player. Because her hearing isn't so good, the way the interview would work is I would ask a question in what I thought was a loud voice, and Linda would shout it out even louder.
<v Ira Glass>那个叫琳达·卡彭特的女人带我回到了那间看起来空无一人的房子。屋里昏暗处，一张扶手椅上坐着尼阿姨，正在做某种杂志上的拼图。尼阿姨的全名是科尼利娅·赖特·纽曼。尼阿姨告诉我，是的，她还是个小女孩时就见过罗斯福总统。FDR很喜欢她父亲的小提琴。因为她听力不太好，采访的方式是我用自认为很大的声音提问，然后琳达再大声地喊给她听。

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:50.830
<v Dave Kestenbaum>So Sylvia told me that FDR used to come here to the Cove to buy moonshine.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>西尔维娅告诉我，FDR以前常来海湾买私酿威士忌。

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:56.080
<v Linda Carpenter>Did FDR come to the Cove to buy moonshine?
<v Linda Carpenter>FDR来过海湾买私酿威士忌吗？

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:12.750
<v Cornelia  Wright Newman>To buy moonshine? Yeah he'd come down to Uncle Charlie's old store, Gilbert's store down there. And that's where he met Daddy.
<v Cornelia  Wright Newman>买私酿威士忌？是的，他会来查理叔叔的老杂货店，就是下面的吉尔伯特店。他在那里遇到了我爸爸。

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:22.120
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Did he buy moonshine? Did FDR buy moonshine there? Did he buy moonshine in Uncle Charlie's old store? FDR?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>他买过私酿威士忌吗？FDR在那里买过私酿威士忌吗？他在查理叔叔的老店买过私酿威士忌吗？FDR？

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:43.230
<v Cornelia  Wright Newman>Yeah, yeah. He was over there one time when he'd come by, he was over there to get his groceries from Uncle Charlie's. And he stopped and he found out where Daddy lived and he'd only played the fiddle. And he'd come on over there.
